      "shortest": true,
      "solution": "n = int(input())\ngrid = [input().split() for i in range(n)]\n\n#find pieces\npieces = []\nfor row in range(n):\n    for col in range(n):\n        if grid[row][col] in ['X','O']: pieces += [[row,col]]\n\n#find intersecting cells\ncell1 = grid[pieces[0][0]][pieces[1][1]]\ncell2 = grid[pieces[1][0]][pieces[0][1]]\n\n#place piece\nif int(cell1)>int(cell2): grid[pieces[0][0]][pieces[1][1]] = 'X'\nelse: grid[pieces[1][0]][pieces[0][1]] = 'X'\n\n\nprint('\\n'.join(' '.join(i) for i in grid))",
      "statement": "On a tic-tac-toe board of size [[n]], two players have each placed a piece such that neither piece shares a row or column. The best move in this circumstance is always to place the next piece on one of the two cells that shares its row with one piece and its column with the other.\n\nGiven the current state of the board, where the placed pieces are marked {{X}} or {{O}} and the remaining cells are represented by their priority values (higher value = higher priority), find the best cell on which to place the next piece, tie breaking based on priority. Return the board after placing {{X}} in that cell.",
